  "explanation": {
    "what": "Plain language: donor checks protect the donor and make sure the blood is good enough to help someone else. Scientific terms (US FDA, 21 CFR 630.10/630.15): temperature ≤ 37.5 °C; BP systolic 90–180, diastolic 50–100 mmHg; pulse regular 50–100/min (physician exception possible); weight ≥ 50 kg; Hb/Hct — female allogeneic ≥ 12.5 g/dL or ≥ 38%, male allogeneic ≥ 13.0 g/dL or ≥ 39%, autologous ≥ 11.0 g/dL or ≥ 33%; whole blood/single apheresis RBC no more than once in 8 weeks, two-unit apheresis RBC once in 16 weeks.",
    "why": "Hemoglobin minimums assure donor safety and product potency; vital signs screen for illness; intervals allow red-cell recovery.",
    "measure": "Hb or Hct by fingerstick, venipuncture or equivalent method — earlobe blood not acceptable. Female donors with Hb 12.0–12.5 g/dL (Hct 36–38%) only under an FDA-acceptable procedure. Proof of identity and a postal address for contact for 8 weeks.",
    "differs": "Allogeneic (sex-specific minimums) vs autologous (≥ 11.0 g/dL). Single-unit (8 weeks) vs two-unit RBC apheresis (16 weeks).",
    "examAngle": "Expect: “can this donor give today?” with Hb, BP, pulse, temperature, weight and interval values."
  },
